# Whether a compiled require/ensure/assert clause is checked
# (docs/spec/values-and-memory.md 2.4). This is the one place that decides
# it; only the lowering of a contract clause reads it, and no contract
# refers to it.
#
# which: "require", "ensure" or "assert"; in_runtime: whether the clause is
# in one of the runtime's own files; runtime_ensures: whether the build asked
# for the runtime's ensures (--check-runtime).
#
# Every clause of a program's own files is checked. In the runtime's files
# the requires and asserts are, since they guard what callers pass in; the
# ensures, which restate what the runtime's own code has just done, are
# checked when the build asks. Checking them all made the runtime's clauses
# over half of a build's time: 1.2 billion clause runs in a front-end build
# of the interpreter, spread over the smallest, most called functions
# (review finding O7).
make a function called v2_contracts_checked takes which, in_runtime, runtime_ensures returns yes
  if in_runtime and (which == "ensure") then
    return runtime_ensures
  end
  return true
end
